          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Betty Friedan's Early Life</strong><br>\nBetty Friedan was born on February 4, 1921, in Peoria, Illinois. Her parents were Jewish immigrants, and her father owned a jewelry store. From a young age, Betty showed a keen interest in philosophy and psychology, which she later pursued in college. Despite facing the challenges of the Great Depression during her youth, she excelled academically and was active in journalism as a student.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Education and Personal Life</strong><br>\nFriedan attended Smith College, graduating in 1942 with a degree in psychology. After college, she won a fellowship to attend the University of California, Berkeley, for graduate work. However, she turned down the fellowship to pursue a career in journalism. She married Carl Friedan in 1947, but they divorced in 1969. The marriage produced three children.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Career Beginnings</strong><br>\nAfter World War II, Friedan returned to New York and worked as a freelance writer for various publications. She wrote about various topics but often faced discrimination in her career. Many of her articles were published under a male pseudonym or anonymously, which was a common practice for female writers at the time.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
