            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>\"The Feminine Mystique\"</strong><br>\nIn 1963, Betty Friedan published \"The Feminine Mystique,\" a book that challenged the traditional roles of women in society. The book quickly became a bestseller and is often credited with sparking the second wave of feminism in the United States. It highlighted \"the problem that has no name\" — the dissatisfaction of many housewives with their limited role in society.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Impact on Women's Rights</strong><br>\nThe Feminine Mystique led many women to question their own satisfaction with homemaker roles. Friedan's work provided a language and framework for discussing women's lack of fulfillment outside of family and home. The book's impact was profound, inspiring women to seek opportunities and rights equal to those of men.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Founding the National Organization for Women (NOW)</strong><br>\nIn 1966, Friedan co-founded the National Organization for Women (NOW), which became one of the leading groups in the women's rights movement. As NOW's first president, she was instrumental in setting the agenda for legal and social changes to support women's equality.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
