          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Later Works and Influence</strong><br>\nBetty Friedan continued to write and advocate for women's rights throughout her life. She published several more books, including \"The Second Stage,\" which addressed the need for revised feminist goals, and \"The Fountain of Age,\" which examined aging and its challenges. Her writings have influenced multiple generations.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Opposition and Controversy</strong><br>\nDespite her contributions, Friedan's work was not without controversy. She was criticized by some feminists for focusing primarily on the needs of middle-class white women and not enough on issues affecting women of color or lower socioeconomic statuses. Friedan's approach to feminism was also considered to be more conservative by radical feminists.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Death</strong><br>\nBetty Friedan's influence on the feminist movement lasted beyond her active years. She witnessed significant changes in women's rights and lived to see many of her advocacy efforts come to fruition. Friedan died on February 4, 2006, on her 85th birthday, leaving behind a legacy as a pioneering feminist and author.</p>\n"
              },
